 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Inside German's zombie horror story, Max shows Senya and Fedya where Lyova bit his arm, right as an emergency news broadcast announces that a mysterious virus has locked down Moscow.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Представьте, меня сейчас Лёва укусил.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Неизвестная инфекция охватила столицу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Все выезды из Москвы перекрыты.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Говорят, вирус прибыл из Таиланда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Вечно туристы всякую гадость тащат!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Назад, не подходи к нам!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Лёва человек очень воспитанный.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Он просто так никого не кусает.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Значит, ты уже инфицирован, Макс!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ребята, да я просто пошутил!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вы что, правда поверили?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Там странный гость мозги заказал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "У нас есть свежие мозги?",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's break down each line of the zombie outbreak in the kitchen.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Представьте, меня сейчас Лёва укусил.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Imagine, Lyova just bit me.' Putting the object pronoun 'me' before the subject highlights what happened to the speaker.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Неизвестная инфекция охватила столицу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'An unknown infection has gripped the capital.' News broadcasts use formal vocabulary like 'gripped/engulfed the capital'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Все выезды из Москвы перекрыты.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'All exits out of Moscow are blocked.' The plural short-form participle 'blocked/shut' agrees with the plural noun 'exits'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Говорят, вирус прибыл из Таиланда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'They say the virus arrived from Thailand.' Formal news reports use the verb 'arrived' rather than everyday 'came'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Вечно туристы всякую гадость тащат!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Tourists are always dragging all kinds of nasty stuff back!' The adverb 'eternally' is used colloquially to mean 'always' when complaining.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Назад, не подходи к нам!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Back, do not come near us!' Senya grabs a weapon as soon as he connects the news report to Max's bite.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Лёва человек очень воспитанный.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Lyova is a very well-mannered person.' The adjective literally means 'well-brought-up' or 'polite'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Он просто так никого не кусает.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'He does not bite anyone for no reason.' The two-word phrase 'simply so' is the everyday Russian idiom for 'for no reason'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Значит, ты уже инфицирован, Макс!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'That means you are already infected, Max!' A masculine short-form participle states Max's condition.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ребята, да я просто пошутил!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Guys, I was just joking!' Backed into a corner, Max desperately claims the bite was just a joke.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вы что, правда поверили?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What, did you really believe it?' The verb 'to believe' is in the plural past tense.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Там странный гость мозги заказал.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A strange guest out there ordered brains.' In Russian culinary terms, 'brains' is always used in the plural.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "У нас есть свежие мозги?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do we have any fresh brains?' Max's sudden craving gives away that the zombie virus is already working. Now listen to the whole zombie outbreak scene again at natural speed.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
